<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-arithmetic-of-conditionally-convergent-series" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Arithmetic of conditionally convergent series
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      We will shortly develop tools which can be used to prove that termwise algebraic combinations of absolutely convergent series converge. However, the same cannot be said of conditionally convergent series.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the termwise sum of two conditionally convergent series need not be conditionally convergent.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <hint>
      <p>
        If <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> is conditionally convergent, what about <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ -x_{ n } } </m>?
      </p>
    </hint>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> be a conditionally convergent series of real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R </m>. We will argue that <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }{ \infty }{ -x_{ n } } </m> is conditionally convergent. Indeed, let <m> \of{ S_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> and <m> \of{ T_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be the sequences of partial sums
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            S_{ n } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ x_{ k } } \amp T_{ n } = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ -x_{ k } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        We observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ -x_{ n } } \amp = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ T_{ n } } = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ -x_{ k } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ -\sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ x_{ k } } } = -\lim_{ n \to \infty }{ S_{ n } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = -\sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } }
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        so the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ -x_{ n } } </m> converges. We observe that the positive part
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \of{ -x_{ n } }^{ + } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ - } } = \infty
        </md>
        
        diverges to <m> \infty </m>, and so this series converges conditionally. We now observe that
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } + \of{ -x_{ n } } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ 0 } = 0
        </md>
        
        converges absolutely.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the termwise scalar multiple of a conditionally convergent series need not be conditionally convergent.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> be a conditionally convergent series of real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R </m>. We observe that
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ 0 x_{ n } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ 0 } = 0
        </md>
        
        converges absolutely.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>
